<p>The U.S. Federal Reserve left its benchmark interest rate unchanged at 3.50% to 3.75% on Wednesday, its third straight hold. The decision came at Jerome Powell’s final scheduled policy meeting as chair before his term ends May 15. Powell said he would remain on the Fed’s Board of Governors “for a period of time to be determined,” under a separate term that runs until 2028. Powell said his decision was tied to legal actions and pressure on the central bank rather than criticism from elected officials, framing the move as a defense of Fed independence. The announcement drew new criticism from President Trump and Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent as Kevin Warsh, Trump’s nominee to replace Powell as chair, moved closer to confirmation. The Fed’s 8-4 policy vote was its most divided decision since October 1992, with three officials opposing wording that still pointed toward possible future rate cuts and one dissenting in favor of an immediate quarter-point cut.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/Sa-7MHB8gY5ZxYieKpIM90HiYsElu8V82MBf9rxtzAz9OwCa0oXpU99ULXnrQCzd5SkkNhnlpHzBrIewCch-slAEFogvdnTH6fy3ga5uJ-9MQcbUyJltivn9Ju3xEjmupX3SHy89DsmnGXtQ4kEJ_Rm3ArBTiwhQy-JiGXXCwkOrNrPau21jbVwpLJJlMCli-GCyC_PflOvxpM7j3WVFYFor2_H_Z80oo0tTY_4ELs-9RxcJHFTgLjzs-Q' alt='Federal Reserve Chairman Jerome Powell speaks at a news conference at the Federal Reserve following the Federal Open Market Committee meeting in Washington, Wednesday, April 29, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Inflation language: The Fed’s statement said inflation was “elevated,” partly because of a recent rise in global energy prices, a change from prior wording that inflation was “somewhat” elevated.</li><li>Named dissenters: Beth Hammack, Neel Kashkari and Lorie Logan supported holding rates but objected to retaining an easing bias, while Stephen Miran dissented in favor of a 25-basis-point cut.</li><li>War context: Powell said the U.S.-Israel war with Iran and disruptions tied to the Strait of Hormuz had added uncertainty to the economic outlook and complicated inflation forecasts.</li><li>Investigation status: Powell said he intended to remain until the Justice Department probe involving Fed building renovations was “well and truly over,” after reports that the department had dropped its criminal investigation a week earlier.</li></ul><blockquote>I worry that these attacks are battering the institution and putting at risk the thing that really matters to the public, which is the ability to conduct monetary policy without taking into consideration political factors - Jerome Powell</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>President Trump: Trump wrote on Truth Social that Jerome “Too Late” Powell wanted to stay at the Fed because he “can&#x27;t get a job anywhere else” and that “Nobody wants him.” (<a href='https://news.rthk.hk/rthk/en/component/k2/1852957-20260430.htm'>RTHK</a>)</li><li>Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent: Bessent told Fox Business that Powell’s decision violated Federal Reserve norms and insulted incoming chair Kevin Warsh. (<a href='https://www.letemps.ch/articles/la-fed-laisse-ses-taux-directeurs-inchanges-pour-la-3e-fois-d-affilee-jerome-powell-annonce-qu-il-restera-comme-gouverneur'>Le Temps</a>)</li><li>Senate Banking Committee Democrats: Semafor reported that all Democrats except Sen. Raphael Warnock skipped the committee vote on Warsh’s nomination, which advanced on a party-line basis. (<a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/04/29/2026/powell-vows-to-stay-at-fed-as-a-governor'>Semafor</a>)</li><li>Jerome Powell: Powell said he would keep a low profile as a governor after Warsh takes over and would leave when he considered it appropriate. (<a href='https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-04-29/powell-says-he-ll-stay-on-as-governor-after-term-as-chair-ends'>Bloomberg</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.wsws.org/en/articles/2026/04/30/omdc-a30.html'>Fed keeps interest rates on hold as Powell declares he will stay on as